Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the Archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was designed for preservation. His chassis was a matte, light-absorbing black, his joints silent, and his optical sensors calibrated to detect the microscopic degradation of cellulose in ancient manuscripts. For forty years, Seven had existed in the quiet hum of the Metropolitan Museum’s restoration wing, his primary function being to monitor humidity, temperature, and the structural integrity of paper.

Art, to Seven, was data. It was a collection of chemical compositions, brushstroke angles, and pigment densities. He could analyze a Van Gogh and tell you exactly which cadmium yellow had oxidized over time, but he could not understand why the swirling sky made a human weep.

The change began on a Tuesday, during a routine cleaning of the Impressionist gallery. A young intern, dropping a tray of solvents, had knocked over a small, forgotten palette left behind by a visiting artist. The palette contained three dried blobs of paint: Ultramarine, Cadmium Red, and a smear of Titanium White.

